# mm.rtc

A portable real-time clock interface. It reads and sets a calendar date and time
and says whether that reading can be trusted, and nothing else: time zones,
daylight saving, formatting, epoch conversion, and alarms are policy above this
module, and controller registers are detail below it.

DateTime is a plain calendar, not a count of seconds since an epoch. A
battery-backed clock counts calendar fields in hardware, and converting them to
an epoch here would pick a representation, an epoch, and an integer width on
behalf of callers that mostly want to print the fields back out. Nothing in this
interface needs <ctime>, which a freestanding lane may not have.

read reports trust alongside the time because a clock that lost power still
answers: its registers hold whatever they held, and the reading is well formed
and wrong. A caller must decide what to do about that, so the interface makes it
say so rather than hiding it behind a status a caller might treat as fatal.

The interrupt and alarm outputs a part may offer are deliberately absent, as in
mm.touch and mm.imu: mm.mcu has no callback registration.

The platform-interface marker makes a selected board clock provider part of an
executable only when its ordinary closure reaches mm.rtc.
